<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Himera</h1><p class="st-meta">648 BCE – 409 BCE (approx.) · capital Himera</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Greek city on Sicily's north coast, site of the great Greek victory over Carthage in 480 BCE and destroyed by Carthage in revenge in 409 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zancle">Zancle</a>, joined by exiles from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a>, founded Himera in 648 BCE at the mouth of the Himera river, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily's</a> northern coast. As the westernmost Greek city on that coast, it faced the Phoenician towns of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/panormus">Panormus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solunto">Soloeis</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elymians">Elymians</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Himera spoke a mixture of Chalcidian and Doric Greek and was home to the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stesichorus">Stesichorus</a>. Its tyrant Terillus was expelled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theron-of-acragas">Theron of Acragas</a> around 483 BCE and appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. In 480 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelon">Gelon of Syracuse</a> and Theron crushed the Carthaginian army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-i-of-carthage">Hamilcar</a> outside the city, and the Victory Temple was built in thanks. Theron's son then governed Himera, and later it was a democracy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 409 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal-mago">Hannibal Mago</a>, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-i-of-carthage">Hamilcar</a>, took Himera, razed it and is said to have executed 3,000 prisoners on the spot where his grandfather had died. Survivors and Carthaginian settlers founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermae-himeraeae">Thermae Himeraeae</a> nearby in 407 BCE. The ruins and battlefield cemeteries have been excavated.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/himera.jpg" alt="Himera" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Clemensfranz · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Himera_Viktoriatempel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>648 BCE – 409 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Himera</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Terillus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-528&amp;focus=himera" aria-label="Himera on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5025 813 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Himera lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5345 1069L5259 1106"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5345 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5345 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-528&amp;focus=himera">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

